          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient has a complete right cranial nerve III palsy. Which of the following m</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient has a complete right cranial nerve III palsy. Which of the following muscles would still function normally in the right eye?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Superior rectus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The superior rectus is innervated by cranial nerve III and would be paralysed in a complete CN III palsy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lateral rectus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The lateral rectus is supplied by cranial nerve VI (abducens). The mnemonic LR6SO4 assigns lateral rectus to CN VI and superior oblique to CN IV; all remaining extraocular muscles are CN III.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inferior oblique</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The inferior oblique is one of the four extraocular muscles innervated by cranial nerve III and would be affected.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Levator palpebrae superioris</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The levator palpebrae superioris is also innervated by CN III, which is why a complete CN III palsy causes ptosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The lateral rectus (CN VI) would still function. Remember: LR6, SO4 — lateral rectus is cranial nerve six, superior oblique is cranial nerve four. All other extraocular muscles, plus the levator and pupillary sphincter, are cranial nerve three."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. LR6, SO4, rest are three — this mnemonic anchors the entire examination of extraocular movements."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
