          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 55-year-old woman presents with acute right-sided headache, ptosis, and a fixe</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 55-year-old woman presents with acute right-sided headache, ptosis, and a fixed dilated pupil. Her right eye rests in a 'down and out' position. What is the most urgent next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Prescribe analgesics and review in one week</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A painful CN III palsy with pupil involvement is a neurosurgical emergency. Delaying risks rupture of a posterior communicating artery aneurysm.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Check blood glucose and reassure if she is diabetic</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Diabetic microvascular CN III palsy is characteristically pupil-sparing. Pupil involvement with pain demands urgent imaging to exclude an aneurysm.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Order CT angiography to exclude a posterior communicating artery aneurysm</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The parasympathetic pupillary fibres run on the surface of CN III and are compressed early by an expanding PComA aneurysm, causing a fixed dilated pupil. This is a neurosurgical emergency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Perform the Bielschowsky three-step test</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The Bielschowsky three-step test confirms cranial nerve IV (trochlear) palsy causing vertical diplopia, not a CN III palsy with ptosis and a down-and-out eye.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is urgent CT angiography. A painful CN III palsy with a fixed dilated pupil must be treated as a PComA aneurysm until excluded. Pupil-sparing CN III palsy in a diabetic suggests microvascular disease — but pupil involvement changes the urgency entirely."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Pain plus pupil involvement in a CN III palsy means posterior communicating artery aneurysm until proven otherwise."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
