          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 5-year-old girl has right esotropia with amblyopia. Full cycloplegic refractio</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 5-year-old girl has right esotropia with amblyopia. Full cycloplegic refraction has been prescribed, but significant deviation persists. What is the next step in management?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediate surgical correction of the squint</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Surgery is step three and should only follow after both refractive correction and amblyopia treatment have been completed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Patching or atropine penalisation of the better eye</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. After refractive correction (step one), amblyopia treatment by penalising the better eye is step two. This must begin before the critical period closes at around seven to eight years.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Repeat refraction without cycloplegic drops</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Non-cycloplegic refraction underestimates hypermetropia in children because accommodation masks the true error. The cycloplegic measurement is correct.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reassess at age eight when the visual system matures</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Waiting until age eight would waste the critical period for treating amblyopia. After this window, the visual loss becomes permanent.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is amblyopia treatment (patching or atropine). The management ladder is: step one — full cycloplegic refraction; step two — penalise the better eye to treat amblyopia; step three — surgery only if deviation persists after both. Treatment must begin before the critical period closes at around age seven to eight."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The three-step ladder — correct, penalise, then operate — protects the child's visual development during the critical period."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
