          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with headache and reduced visual acuity. Acuity does not impr</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with headache and reduced visual acuity. Acuity does not improve when tested through a pinhole. What is the most appropriate interpretation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The patient has uncorrected hypermetropia requiring spectacles</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In hypermetropia, the pinhole would improve acuity by reducing the blur circle. No improvement through the pinhole points to a cause beyond refractive error.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The cause is likely organic, such as macular disease or optic neuropathy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The pinhole eliminates refractive error by narrowing the cone of light entering the eye. If acuity does not improve, the pathology lies in the retina or optic nerve, not in optics.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The pinhole test is unreliable and cycloplegic refraction is needed instead</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The pinhole test is a reliable bedside screen. No improvement is a clinically significant finding that warrants further investigation for organic pathology.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The patient has presbyopia and needs reading glasses</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Presbyopia causes difficulty with near vision specifically and would show improvement through a pinhole or with plus lenses. Failure to improve suggests a non-refractive cause.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is an organic cause. The pinhole works by eliminating refractive blur. If acuity still does not improve, the problem is not optical — it lies in the macula, optic nerve, or visual pathway and requires further investigation."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Pinhole improvement points to a refractive cause; no improvement points to organic pathology — investigate further."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
